                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Watson",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "16e16178-e972-3875-a809-d1a7647f28a1",
                                        "entry_id": "dd659e5a-daad-34f2-b67b-79f31535f5a0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A northern English and Scottish surname originating as a patronymic."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5b40a548-dc9b-4dfb-a5c4-4e5158ad4d41",
                                                "sense_id": "16e16178-e972-3875-a809-d1a7647f28a1",
                                                "text": "Albert Bigelow Paine, his biographer to be and literary executor, who has been constantly with him, said that for the last year at least Mr. Clemens had been weary of life. When Richard Watson Gilder died, he said: \"How fortunate he is. No good fortune of that kind ever comes to me.\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8d301ff7-6609-3f1e-ac56-b207c25749f3",
                                        "entry_id": "dd659e5a-daad-34f2-b67b-79f31535f5a0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any character who acts as a catalyst for the detective protagonist's mental processes in a mystery story; a consciousness that is privy to facts in the case without being in on the conclusions drawn from them until the proper time. After William L. DeAndrea, discussing Sir Arthur Conan Doyle."
                                        ],
